From 2056a78af28adb31abb8c38f8996cb644a386ba9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Cipoletti <adam@amcreativecoach.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 15:25:22 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Engine: what nine years of compiler does to a 2017 codebase

Building WebKit 604 with GCC 14 instead of the GCC 6.5 this port was
written against, so that what a newer compiler is worth here can be
measured rather than guessed at.

Seven headers it no longer supplies by accident - <cstdint>, <utility>
and <functional> among them, the last inside the __cplusplus guard the
declarations already use, because plain Objective-C files read the same
headers. Three linker options it forwards differently: -umbrella WebKit
and its kind arrive as two arguments unless spelled -Wl,-umbrella,WebKit,
and the build stops on "file not found: WebKit". Two inline definitions
it declines to emit, wanting the defining header in view. Four functions
and constants the 10.5 SDK marks as 10.6-and-later, which makes them
unavailable rather than absent: a soft link cannot help, because the name
it defines is the name the SDK has spoken for, so they are looked up
under names of their own.

And one internal compiler error, on one file, which -fno-tree-pre does
not avoid despite the crash being in that pass. That file is built at -O1
instead, and only when the compiler is GCC 14, so that comparing the two
compilers is not quietly comparing one fewer optimization everywhere.
